From 55fa78de9b9fc48cdb709c0010b27299dde7d1ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 10:53:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] robo(slabs): compute S.No. 1..N per batch from slab-number
 order, not the stored value
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

S.No. was read straight off the stored serialNumber — the number the operator
typed (or the old paper register's own column, on an import). On old runs those
were mistyped: a batch would go 1…36 then restart at 24, so the download showed
duplicate S.No. and appeared to stop short of the real slab count (a 92-slab
batch ending at 36). The Batch Numbers were mistyped alongside them, so the
S.No. cannot be repaired from the S.No.

S.No. is now DERIVED, everywhere it is shown, from the one authoritative order in
the register: the physical SLAB NUMBER. Slabs are numbered by the plant as they
are produced, so within a batch, ascending slab number is the production
sequence — rank by it and the S.No. is a clean 1…N by construction. This fixes
the old mistyped runs and every run from here on with no column to keep in step;
the stored serialNumber is left untouched in the database, just no longer shown.

  • New pure, tested helper slabSequence.ts (compareSlabOrder / sequencedByBatch
    / sequenceNumbersById): numeric slab-number order, createdAt+id only breaking
    ties, deterministic.
  • Complete Production download (productionGrouping.ts): both layouts number
    each batch 1…N in slab-number order — batch mode no longer prints the stored
    S.No., date mode numbers each group the same way.
  • Slab detail page: S.No. is this slab's rank within its batch.
  • Slabs Records: a new S.No. column, computed server-side and shown when a
    Batch No. is searched (the whole batch is in view); "-" for a partial set.

Applies to old and new records uniformly — no data migration, no schema change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
